From 377008e318a9b5d73d49722513d348fe69b18cc1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Christopher Owen <3221756+christopherowen@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 16 Oct 2026 20:59:10 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] DeepSeek-V4.1 Engram: interleave disk shards' hash columns
 across ranks

Heads 0-7 hash 2-grams, 8-15 3-grams and 16-23 4-grams, and a longer n-gram
repeats less, so a step reads more distinct rows of it (1 : 1.35 : 1.54 per
4096-token chunk of source text). Contiguous columns gave TP3's rank 2 every
4-gram head and the slowest reads (first prefill chunk 74.8 ms against rank 0's
51.6). Rank r now reads every tp-th column from r (TP3 3/3/2 heads per order,
TP4 2/2/2): the reader and the gather take a 24-bit column mask, and the forward
gathers each rank's columns and puts them back in column order with one
transpose (exact copies, the same rows). The debug timeline records each step's
planning time (plan_ns).
